    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah.jpg" alt="Ras al-Khaimah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_RAK_City_from_Al_Qawasim_Corniche_flagpole.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stevenmccombe, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sharjah grew up on a sheltered creek on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, in a region of fishing and pearling settlements, date gardens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> pastures. It became a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Qawasim</a>, or Al Qasimi family, the leaders of a seafaring tribal confederation whose main centre in the eighteenth century was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a>, further up the coast. The Qawasim are traditionally said to have established their rule at Sharjah in 1727, the date used for the sheikhdom’s beginning, although early details are poorly recorded. In the later eighteenth century the Qawasim built a substantial maritime power, with fleets that carried trade and fought rivals on both shores of the Gulf, and branches of the family ruled ports on the Persian coast, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-lengeh">Lingeh</a>. The decisive figure in Sharjah’s rise was Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-bin-saqr-al-qasimi">Sultan bin Saqr Al Qasimi</a>, who became head of the Qawasim in 1803 and ruled, with interruptions, until his death in 1866. After British naval expeditions destroyed much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> al-Khaimah’s fleet and fortifications in 1809 and 1819, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> bin Saqr made Sharjah the principal seat of the family. From there he governed Ras al-Khaimah and a string of dependencies along the coast and in the interior, making Sharjah for much of the nineteenth century the leading Qasimi town on what the British came to call the Trucial Coast.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mumbai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mumbai.jpg" alt="Mumbai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumbai_Bandra-Worli_Sea_Link.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rutiknatekar, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Life in Sharjah was shaped by the sea and the desert. The town’s main wealth came from pearling: every summer fleets of wooden dhows sailed to the pearl banks of the Gulf, where divers worked for months under the command of captains, the <em>nakhuda</em>, who in turn depended on advances from merchants. Debts often bound divers and their families to the same captains and financiers for years. The pearls were sold through merchants to markets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and beyond, and the trade linked Sharjah to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. Fishing, boatbuilding and coastal shipping supported much of the rest of the population, while date palms were cultivated in oases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaid">Dhaid</a> in the interior, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes grazed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and goats and owed loose allegiance to the ruler. Sharjah also controlled the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khor-fakkan">Khor Fakkan</a> and, for most of the period, Kalba on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-oman">Gulf of Oman</a> coast. The population was Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim, with communities of Persian, Baluchi and Indian merchants and workers, and enslaved Africans worked in pearling and households until the trade was suppressed under British pressure. In the 1930s the pearl industry collapsed under the twin blows of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> and Japanese cultured pearls, bringing hardship, while the silting of Sharjah’s creek helped neighbouring Dubai overtake it as the coast’s main commercial port.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sharjah-fort"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sharjah-fort.jpg" alt="Sharjah Fort" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sharjah-fort">Sharjah Fort</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sharjah_Fort_(Al_Hisn).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexandermcnabb, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Qawasim</a> were the most formidable naval power of the lower Gulf around 1800, and their attacks on shipping, including vessels under British protection, led the British authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to describe them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>. Many historians now see their warfare as part of wider commercial and political rivalries in the Gulf, including the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and the Wahhabi movement, with which the Qawasim were then aligned. British expeditions sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> in 1809 and again in 1819, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a> was taken and Qasimi vessels and forts along the coast were destroyed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-maritime-treaty-of-1820">General Maritime Treaty of 1820</a>, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-bin-saqr-al-qasimi">Sultan bin Saqr</a> among others, banned piracy and maritime raiding, and a Maritime Truce first concluded in 1835 and made perpetual in 1853 forbade warfare at sea between the rulers. Conflict on land continued: Sharjah fought its neighbours over grazing grounds, towns and the loyalty of tribes, and in 1940 there was a brief war between Sharjah and Dubai. Defence rested on the ruler’s retainers, armed townsmen and allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a>, supported by watchtowers and forts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharjah-fort">Al Hisn</a> fort in Sharjah town. In 1951 the British formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-oman-scouts">Trucial Oman Levies</a>, later the Trucial Oman Scouts, with their headquarters at Sharjah, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> kept a station at the Sharjah airfield until 1971.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khor-fakkan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khor-fakkan.jpg" alt="Khor Fakkan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khor-fakkan">Khor Fakkan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_best_Hiking_path_in_UAE_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ashiq pang, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sharjah was ruled by sheikhs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Al Qasimi</a> family, chosen from among the ruler’s relatives by a combination of descent, family agreement and the support of leading men and tribes. Successions were often violent, and rulers could be deposed or killed by brothers, nephews or cousins. The sheikh governed in person, sitting in his <em>majlis</em> to hear petitions and settle disputes according to Islamic law and tribal custom, and he relied on relatives and appointed governors, or <em>walis</em>, to administer dependencies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khor-fakkan">Khor Fakkan</a> and Kalba. Income came from taxes on pearling boats, customs duties and date gardens. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> shaped the sheikhdom’s external position. After the treaties of 1820, 1835 and 1853, the Exclusive Agreement of 1892 bound the ruler not to deal with any foreign power except Britain or to cede territory without British consent. Britain was represented on the coast by a Residency Agent, based in Sharjah for much of the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. British recognition also defined which rulers counted as independent: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> al-Khaimah was separate from 1869 to 1900 and finally from 1921, Fujairah broke away from 1902 and was recognised in 1952, and Kalba was recognised as a separate sheikhdom in 1936 before rejoining Sharjah in 1951. From 1952 the rulers met in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states-council">Trucial States Council</a> under British chairmanship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mahatta-fort"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mahatta-fort.jpg" alt="Al Mahatta Fort" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mahatta-fort">Al Mahatta Fort</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Mahatta_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexandermcnabb, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As the main seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Qawasim</a> in the nineteenth century, Sharjah stood at the centre of the relationship between the Gulf Arab sheikhdoms and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. The treaties its rulers signed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1820 onwards defined the system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a>, under which local dynasties kept internal authority while Britain controlled their foreign relations and maritime affairs, a system that shaped the political map of the lower Gulf until 1971. The debate over Qasimi piracy remains a subject of historical controversy, and the later ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-bin-muhammad-al-qasimi">Sultan bin Muhammad Al Qasimi</a> published <em>The Myth of Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">Piracy</a> in the Gulf</em> in 1986, challenging the British account. Sharjah was also the first place on the Trucial Coast connected to modern air travel. In 1932 the British airline <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-airways">Imperial Airways</a> opened a rest house and airfield there on its route between Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the fortified rest house, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mahatta-fort">Al Mahatta</a>, is now a museum. The airfield, later an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">RAF</a> base, brought employment, cash income and contact with the outside world during the collapse of pearling, and Sharjah was for a time the main British administrative and military centre on the coast. Many families from Sharjah and its dependencies later played prominent roles in the federation’s political and commercial life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saqr-bin-sultan-al-qasimi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saqr-bin-sultan-al-qasimi.jpg" alt="Saqr bin Sultan Al Qasimi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saqr-bin-sultan-al-qasimi">Saqr bin Sultan Al Qasimi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheik-Saqr-bin-Sultan-al-Qasimi-Flag-and-Map_1963_stamps_of_Sharjah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Government of Sharjah, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the twentieth century Sharjah’s position declined relative to its neighbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a> and Fujairah slipped from its control, the creek silted up, and Dubai drew away much of the trade, while oil was found in large quantities first in Abu Dhabi and Dubai. Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqr-bin-sultan-al-qasimi">Saqr bin Sultan</a> (r. 1951–1965) sympathised with Arab nationalism and welcomed contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a>, and in 1965 the British, who regarded his policies as a threat, had him deposed. His cousin Khalid bin Muhammad succeeded him and worked with the other rulers on plans for a federation after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> announced in 1968 that it would withdraw from the Gulf by the end of 1971. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> claimed the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-musa">Abu Musa</a>, which belonged to Sharjah, and in November 1971 Khalid accepted a memorandum of understanding under which Iranian troops were stationed on part of the island while Sharjah kept administration of the rest. On 2 December 1971 Sharjah became one of the six founding members of the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, ending its existence as a separate state under British protection. In January 1972 the deposed Saqr bin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> attempted a coup in which Khalid was killed; the coup failed, and Khalid’s brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-bin-muhammad-al-qasimi">Sultan bin Muhammad Al Qasimi</a> became ruler. Sharjah continues as one of the seven emirates of the UAE.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sharjah was the chief seat of the Al Qasimi dynasty on the Trucial Coast, whose treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1820 helped create the system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a>, a pearling and trading port that hosted the coast’s first airfield and joined the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> as a founding member in 1971.</em></p>

